# Documentation and standards – PandaTrack

When implementing new features or applying changes, follow these two practices so the project stays documented and consistent.

---

## 1. Document in `docs/` when it adds lasting context

Use the `docs/` folder for knowledge that helps future work, onboarding, and product traceability - not only for architecture notes.

### Document in `docs/` when:

- **New module or area**: You add a new subsystem (e.g. auth, payments, a new app section). Create or update a doc that describes structure, entry points, and how to extend it (similar to `docs/development/i18n.md`).
- **Design or architecture decision**: A choice affects how other code should be written (e.g. how we call APIs, how we handle errors in a new layer). Add a short note in an existing doc or a small ADR-style section (what we decided and why).
- **Convention or flow others must follow**: Any pattern that other developers (or the AI) should reuse (e.g. adding a new locale, adding a new page type). Document the steps and where to touch the code.
- **Product behavior changes**: When a feature's user-visible behavior, rules, limits, defaults, or flow changes, update the relevant `docs/product/` file (`PRD`, `FRD`, `Blueprint`, or `Work Order`) in the same change so the documented product matches the shipped behavior.
- **Requested product additions or undocumented decisions**: When the user asks for a new behavior, UX pattern, copy decision, flow detail, or implementation rule that affects the product and that detail is missing from `docs/product`, add it to the owning product doc in the same change even if the user did not explicitly ask for docs updates.
- **Shared utilities inventory changes**: When `src/lib/` changes (new file, removed file, rename, or responsibility changes), update `docs/development/lib-utilities.md` in the same change.
- **Database schema changes**: When `prisma/schema.prisma` changes (new or removed tables or columns, renamed fields, or meaning/purpose of a table or attribute), update `docs/development/database-schema.md` in the same change. That doc describes purpose and usage only; types and constraints stay in the schema.

### Do not push to `docs/` for:

- Single function or component behavior; use **comments and JSDoc** in code (see quality-docs-cleanup rule).
- One-off or feature-specific details that do not affect other parts of the project.

### Product doc selection

- Update the **nearest product doc that owns the behavior**:
- `PRD` for product goals and cross-feature expectations.
- `FRD` for feature requirements and business rules.
- `Blueprint` for system flow boundaries and implementation contracts.
- `Work Order` for concrete slice behavior, acceptance criteria, and execution-level flow details.
- If a behavior change is material at more than one level, update more than one product doc in the same change.
- Do not treat documentation sync as optional or defer it until a later prompt. The agent must proactively review whether the implemented behavior is already specified and must update the relevant product doc(s) before considering the task complete.
- If the behavior is both newly introduced and not yet represented anywhere in `docs/product`, document it at the nearest owning level and propagate upward to additional levels when the new detail affects requirements, business rules, architecture contracts, or product goals.

### Requirement and rule identifiers

- Keep requirement and business-rule IDs sequential and clean inside the document. Do not create suffix variants such as `FR-01-24a` or `BR-01-07a`.
- If the new text is really part of an existing requirement or rule, merge it into that existing item instead of creating a suffixed variant.
- If it is truly a new separate requirement or rule, renumber the following items in that document so the sequence stays consistent.

### Structure in `docs/`:

- **`docs/product/`**: Product source of truth (`PRD`, `FRDs`, `Blueprints`, `Work Orders`).
- **`docs/development/i18n.md`**: i18n setup, structure, and how to add locales or namespaces.
- **`docs/design/README.md`**: design-system index and file-selection guide.
- **`docs/design/visual-foundations.md`**: visual language, semantic design variables, typography, color, spacing, surfaces, and elevation.
- **`docs/design/interface-patterns.md`**: layout hierarchy, tabs, modals, right sidebars, controls, motion, and reusable interface patterns.
- **`docs/development/og-images.md`**: OG image generation conventions and implementation notes.
- **`docs/development/lib-utilities.md`**: app-wide utilities inventory (`src/lib`) and each file responsibility.
- **`docs/development/database-schema.md`**: database tables and attributes (purpose, where used, why it matters); see `prisma/schema.prisma` for types and constraints.
- **`docs/process/`**: Delivery process standards (DoD, review checklist, AI workflow).
- **New areas**: Prefer one focused file per topic and place it in the nearest coherent folder.

When you add or update something in `docs/`, place it in the right file and keep the same style (short sections, code examples where useful, clear headings).

### Path references in docs

- Use repository-relative paths or plain repo paths in documentation.
- Do not use machine-specific absolute filesystem paths such as `/Users/...` because docs must remain portable across environments.

---

## 2. Create a new repository rule only when it is a recurring standard

Not every instruction should become a rule. Create a new rule only when it will guide future implementations in a consistent way.

When the user explicitly describes a pattern they want applied across the project, consider capturing it in a repository rule. That does not always mean creating a new file: depending on the case, it may be better to update an existing rule (e.g. `coding-standards.mdc`, `project-structure.mdc`) to add the new convention, so the rule set stays small and consistent.

### Create a new rule when all of the following are true:

- **Recurring**: It is a pattern that will apply again (how we write or structure code), not a one-off feature request.
- **Future-facing**: It describes how to do things from now on, not a single fix or single feature.
- **Concise and actionable**: It can be written in a short, clear way (ideally under 50 lines) with concrete do/don’t or examples.
- **No duplication**: It does not repeat or contradict an existing rule in `.agents/rules/`. If it extends one, consider updating that rule instead of adding a new file.

### Do not create a new rule when:

- The instruction is about implementing a specific feature or fixing a single bug.
- The same idea is already covered (or could be covered) in an existing rule.
- It is a vague or one-time preference.

### Before adding a rule:

1. Check existing rules (e.g. `coding-standards.mdc`, `quality-docs-cleanup.mdc`, `project-structure.mdc`) to avoid overlap.
2. If in doubt, add a short comment in code or a note in the relevant `docs/` file instead of creating a new rule.
3. New rules live in `.agents/rules/` as `.mdc` files, with a clear `description` in the frontmatter and focused content.

This keeps the rule set small and useful so that documentation and standards stay maintainable.
